  <p class="iop-essay-label">
    WEEK 10 / ESSAY ARCHIVE
  </p>

  <h3>YOU ONLY GET TO PICK TEN</h3>

  <p>
    “What do you value in life? Pick from these worksheets,” the therapist said as she handed out a couple of pages to a bunch of confused looking inmates. I scanned mine as I read all sorts of wonderful words that didn’t belong in a jail. <em>Love, family, peace, fair, sharing, organized, patient, successful</em>… <em>What are these doing here?</em> I wondered to myself. The assignment was to pick from this list: pick our values. We were only supposed to pick a certain number, even though we could’ve picked every single one on the list. I just remember having a hard time picking out only five. We shared our values, and they were pretty similar: most of the girls just wanted peace, love, and kindness.
  </p>

  <p>
    “We’re going to pick our values from these pages,” the therapist said as she showed a couple of pages to a group of eager looking addicts. I scanned the pages as I read all sorts of wonderful words that belonged in IOP. I picked ten, and I remember having a hard time picking only ten. We shared our values, and they were pretty similar: love and kindness.
  </p>

  <p>
    Now, I took a test and revisited those pages and found my values to be:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Family</li>
    <li>Love</li>
    <li>Spirituality</li>
    <li>Peace</li>
    <li>Gratitude</li>
    <li>Patience</li>
    <li>Pursuit of happiness</li>
    <li>Security</li>
    <li>Empathy</li>
    <li>Sharing</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    I found that the results of the different tests I took and revisiting the IOP values pages to be accurate reflections of what I find important. There is a strong emphasis on connections with others within my core values. By looking at my values and seeing how they align closely with how I live and what I put stock in, I can see that core values shape our identities, guide our decisions, and influence how we live our lives. Although values may complement each other or sometimes contradict one another, understanding what matters most to us allows us to make conscious choices, establish priorities, and live more authentically; an important note is that identifying our values is not the same as living by them. There is a big difference.
  </p>

  <p>
    Four of my values are related to others: family, love, empathy, and sharing. Family for me means people who are related to me by blood and people I have chosen as my family such as my best friends and my neighbors. We look out for each other, love each other, help each other, and would do anything for each other. We have those hard conversations and do tough love if we need to—because we care and want the best for each other. (As an addict, I am familiar with tough love).
  </p>

  <p>
    Love is abundant in my family and my chosen family. They all know that I love them because I won’t stop telling them that I do. I love love. I will express it and cultivate meaningful emotional bonds with others. I am an empathetic person—I have been through a lot. I also listen and can understand what a person has experienced. This comes in with my relationships, and it’s something I value because this is apparently something that not everyone can do so I try to practice empathy whenever I can, and I share this with others. Sharing time, things, resources, and other things between friends, family, and chosen family is something that helps bond the relationships as well. Sharing is done freely; there is nothing expected in return. I’ll give a few cigarettes to my neighbor, or I’ll loan some money to a friend.
  </p>

  <p>
    There’re values that express someone relationship with others, and there’re values that express someone’s relationship with the world around them. For me, one of those is spirituality. Since getting sober, my spirituality has become one of the most important things in my life. I personally believe that different spiritual traditions offer valuable perspectives, and the different traditions and teachings of Buddhism, Hinduism, and other religions and philosophies influence my understanding of life. I practice metta, or loving-kindness, prayer, offerings, and I have an altar that I tend to daily. There is a difference between just believing in spiritual principles versus putting them into practice. In my own practice, spirituality has influenced the way I treat myself and others by opening my eyes to how different we are yet we are all similar. Like the stories about the values picked by the women in jail and my group members in IOP, they reflected the same things: they just wanted peace, love, and kindness. They just want to be good people and have good things happen to them. Through Buddhism, I practice compassion for others and prayer.
  </p>

  <p>
    Concerning my value of peace, I found inner peace while I was in jail. I would’ve thought it to be impossible in a place like that, but I found myself untouched by my surroundings and able to remain tranquil. I was practicing radical acceptance and mindfulness in those tough moments as well as studying the Dhammapada, reciting verses to myself. And I was grateful to have that inner peace, despite being incarcerated. At the time, I was practicing gratitude by writing in a journal. Now, I practice gratitude by telling people I am thankful for them and by praying. If I am in a difficult situation, I can still practice gratitude by relying on the teachings of the different religions and philosophies that I follow.
  </p>

  <p>
    Another big lesson I learned in jail that became a key core value to me was patience. I wasn’t passively waiting for my release date—I was working on myself. I was writing in my journal whenever I had the chance, I read 52 books in five months, I went to therapy, I took classes. I was being patient by working. And I believe patience is a skill—it’s necessary to work at being patient. Tying these all together, spirituality emphasizes patience and gratitude, and through practicing those two things you can find peace.
  </p>

  <p>
    The pursuit of happiness is mentioned in the Declaration of Independence along with life and liberty. Happiness is something to be cultivated. To me, happiness takes hard work and is a skill that needs to be honed. It isn’t something that happens overnight, and it doesn’t just happen. It’s not pleasure; pleasure is just a feeling, it’s fleeting. It’s important to be skillfully happy—mindfully pursuing happiness yet considering what the happiness does. Drinking for me was pleasure, but the act of going to a bar to see friends made me happy. That was not a good way to be happy. This ties to security and stability and safety. Feeling secure makes it easier for me to enjoy my life. I can’t easily feel joy if my circumstances are uncertain, but I can find things that will make me happy. There is a balance with security though. They say fortune favors the bold, but I wouldn’t be too bold. Hiding from the world because you want to be completely secure isn’t living—there needs to be a healthy mindset regarding security.
  </p>

  <p>
    Core values can evidently work together, but can they contradict each other yet still work together? My ten core values might not always agree, such as security and pursuit of happiness. One instance of these conflicting values is when I left the US to live in Barcelona for a year as an English teacher. There was a part of me that needed a break from everything I was going through where I was living—and I needed adventure and new experiences, new people. Another part of me was terrified of leaving familiar surroundings to go somewhere that I would have trouble communicating, where I wouldn’t know anybody, and where I would have to really stand on my own two legs. I decided to go, and it was one of the best experiences of my life. I let the security side of me take a backseat because I wanted to do something new and something that I had never tried before. I was still cautious while in Spain and had my security value close to my heart, but I let my sense of adventure and pursuit of happiness drive me to many new things: new friends, skateboarding the streets of Barcelona, exploring a new city, trying new foods, tutoring the most amazing students. At the same time, I made sure I was in safe areas, I knew where I was going, and I wasn’t going to get pickpocketed. I kept my safety a high priority but had fun at the same time.
  </p>

  <p>
    Back to those two rooms—two different groups, but the same values on the pages. Two groups of people in different circumstances chose the same types of values: peace, love, and kindness. It says a lot about us humans, doesn’t it? It speaks to me as someone who is in both groups, someone who has been on the inside and who is on the outside in a therapy group doing the same exercise. We all desire connection, tranquility, and kindheartedness from others. These feelings and principles start within ourselves—at our core.
  </p>

</article>
